4.31.m:
Kāma never acts [against] that favorite of his;
he becomes free from disease, endowed with fortune, and radiant like Kāmadeva;
he obtains a virtuous woman as a wife who enchants the three worlds.

4.32.m:
Directing them, he entered within and wrought passion in him.
A male cuckoo uttered a sweet call near him;
a six-footed bee, standing in front, hummed a lovely, soft note;
the fragrant wind blew unceasingly, gentle and very cool, O beloved;

4.33.m:
"The past, the present, and the future arise from your Māyā."
Having spoken thus, that Brahmā stood in the assembly with shame;
the Lord, the inner ruler of all, devised a remedy for him.
